description:
pkix-chain/src/aia_extract.rs:82-89 (`is_http_scheme`) tests an ASCII-case-insensitive scheme prefix with a hand-rolled closure that zips each byte through .to_ascii_lowercase() and compares with Iterator::eq:

    let lower_prefix = |p: &str| {
        uri.len() > p.len()
            && uri.as_bytes()[..p.len()]
                .iter()
                .map(|b| b.to_ascii_lowercase())
                .eq(p.bytes())
    };
    lower_prefix("http://") || lower_prefix("https://")

Idiomatic Rust uses <[u8]>::eq_ignore_ascii_case (stable since 1.23) or str::eq_ignore_ascii_case. The case-fold-and-zip pattern obscures intent — a reader has to puzzle out that the iterator chain is just an ASCII-case-insensitive byte compare. A direct rewrite is:

    fn is_http_scheme(uri: &str) -> bool {
        let b = uri.as_bytes();
        (b.len() > 7 && b[..7].eq_ignore_ascii_case(b"http://"))
            || (b.len() > 8 && b[..8].eq_ignore_ascii_case(b"https://"))
    }

or, leaning on str::get for bounds-safe indexing:

    fn is_http_scheme(uri: &str) -> bool {
        uri.get(..8).is_some_and(|p| p.eq_ignore_ascii_case("https://"))
            || uri.get(..7).is_some_and(|p| p.eq_ignore_ascii_case("http://"))
    }

Cost of leaving it: clarity and grep-ability. A grep for eq_ignore_ascii_case (a well-known stdlib API) misses this call site; the next person who needs to add a scheme has to re-derive the closure semantics before extending it. Functionally equivalent.
